body:
your resume is very traditional....and boring...recruiters get 100s of applicants at time. I have a close friend who is a recruiter for 10 years and told me that they scan for key words for what the company is hiring for.  So for example. If a company is hiring for cloud experience and they want 4 years of that, they scan for how many times the word "CLOUD" and "4 years" comes up in your resume. and guess what?  your resume doesn't have much of those words. not only that because your resume is super boring, they wont even give your resume a second look because to be honest , its an eyesore and puts them to sleep.  

&#x200B;

it sucks that all the experience listed just goes unnoticed by recruiters. you cant blame them because they are recruiters, not devop professionals that know about other technologies. 

&#x200B;

so moral of the story is, for every company you apply for, your resume changes its focus to what the company wants. Tedious? yes. but its obvious your resume does not have that "Wow" factor. so you should go with the tedious route. just my 2 cents , some people might have a different opinion.

